This license does not require you to publish source code or private changes.

3. Minecraft community permissions

The following are also permitted purposes, including the revenue-generating
activities expressly described below. These specific permissions override
the noncommercial baseline only for these activities; Sections 4 and 5 still
apply.

a. Free modpacks. You may include and redistribute the Software, unmodified
   or permissibly modified, in Minecraft modpacks made available without
   payment for the pack or the Software. Ordinary hosting-platform
   advertising or creator-reward revenue, and genuinely optional donations,
   do not by themselves disqualify a free modpack. Payment, a purchase,
   subscription, or donation must not be required to obtain its copies of
   the Software. This permission does not allow selling modpacks containing
   the Software.

b. Videos and livestreams. You may create, publish, and monetize gameplay
   videos, reviews, tutorials, and livestreams featuring the Software,
   including through advertising, sponsorships, tips, donations, and voluntary
   support subscriptions that do not restrict viewing. Videos and streams
   containing Minecraft content must remain free to view in their entirety
   and comply with the applicable Mojang/Microsoft terms. This license does
   not authorize a viewing paywall or sale of video or stream copies. It
   grants only the Licensor's rights concerning the media, not paid
   distribution of the Software or extraction of its code into another
   product. Other third-party conditions remain applicable under Section 6.

c. Minecraft servers. You may use, copy, modify, and deploy the Software to
   operate and maintain Minecraft servers, including servers that accept
   donations or charge for access, subscriptions, or server services.
   You may freely redistribute copies needed by players. Charging for
   server gameplay or services is allowed; charging for the Software,
   its download, a Software license key, or delivery of a modified version
   is not. Obtaining a copy you distribute must not require a payment,
   purchase, subscription, or donation. Fees solely for access to server
   gameplay or services remain permitted. This permission does not authorize
   selling custom builds, paid ports, or other paid deliveries of modified
   copies, or incorporating the code into unrelated commercial products.

   Employees acting on behalf of a server operator may perform the permitted
   internal operation, maintenance, and modification activities for that
   operator. Payment of wages for those internal activities does not by
   itself constitute a sale or paid delivery of the Software. This does not
   authorize external sales or paid delivery of forks or custom builds.

d. Independent server services. Independent providers may charge for
   infrastructure, hosting, installation, administration, configuration, or
   technical support for Minecraft servers otherwise permitted under Section
   3(c). Such charges must be for those services, not for a Software license
   or access to, download of, or delivery of a Software copy. Obtaining a copy
   must not require purchasing those services. This permission does not
   authorize paid custom development or paid delivery of forks, ports,
   patches, or custom builds. Describing a Software charge as a hosting,
   support, or customization fee does not change that restriction.

4. Uses not authorized by this license

Except under separate written authorization from the applicable rights
holders, you must not:

a. Sell, rent, commercially license, or otherwise charge for copies of the
   unmodified Software, whether standalone, repackaged, or in a paid bundle.

b. Charge for access to, download of, or delivery of modified versions,
   forks, ports, or custom builds. A required donation, subscription, or
   purchase in exchange for such a copy is a charge, whatever it is called.
   Calling a Software charge a service or customization fee does not make
   it an authorized server-service charge.

c. Copy, adapt, port, or incorporate copyright-protected code or other
   protected portions of the Software into commercial software products
   or services, except for the specific Minecraft activities in Section 3.

These restrictions concern rights in the covered Software, not mere ideas,
algorithms, facts, or independently written implementations that do not use
its protected expression. Running an actual Minecraft server under Section
3(c) is not prohibited merely because that server earns revenue.

